WEBVTT

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.270
EDO SEGAL: Welcome to the
Napster Companion API session.

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:04.710
If you're here, you're
probably curious

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.530
about video multimodal agents

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.270
that you can deploy
in your application.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.430
One of the core
areas of friction

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:14.640
for doing this has
been the cost.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.910
We've been working for the last
three years on reducing the cost

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.210
and engineering the heck out of
it, so we can deliver it to you

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.440
at a cost-effective way,

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.680
so you can actually scale
it to millions of users.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.480
And we've also made
it incredibly easy

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.740
for you to deploy them.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.580
So literally within one day, you
can add a video multimodal agent

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.320
to your agentic application.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.890
This adds another layer
of human connection

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.050
to everything you're building,
as we're already seeing

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.300
in the wild with some of the
world's biggest companies.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.480
We'll share some examples, as
well as walk you through how

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.120
to actually build it yourself
in a matter of hours.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.810
Over to Ziv Navoth, our
Chief Product Officer.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.130
ZIV NAVOTH: Thanks, Edo.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.650
Let's start with cost
because for many developers,

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.990
that's been the gating factor.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.510
Real-time multimodal video
agents have been technically

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.280
possible for a while.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.310
What hasn't been possible
is shipping them at scale.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:09.120
The Omniagent API runs at only
one cent per render minute

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.920
when you bring your own LLM.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.550
That's roughly 20 times
cheaper than the alternatives.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:18.570
Enough that a five-minute
customer call costs five cents,

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:22.080
and a full-time agent running
eight hours a day costs less

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.460
than $5.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.850
So that's a shift that makes
this a production tool instead

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.410
of a proof of concept.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.170
Now, let's talk about how
this fits in your stack.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:34.110
The Omniagent API
is Azure Native.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:39.450
The browser-side SDK opens an
HTTPS and WebRTC connection

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.110
through Azure's Front Door
and load balancing solutions.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:46.980
Our network stack also has
private endpoint support.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:51.660
Omniagent core pods run in Azure
Kubernetes Service, auto-scaled.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.310
The orchestration layer can talk

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:55.860
to your own Azure
OpenAI deployment,

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.540
or it can talk to ours.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.760
The real-time multimodal
rendering pipeline runs

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.950
on dedicated Azure VM clusters

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.100
and streams synchronized
video back over WebRTC

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.500
with low round-trip latency.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.840
Every component you see
in this stack is running

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.840
on an Azure managed service.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.420
Omniagent delivers three
additional benefits.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.000
First, single cloud billing.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.270
The line item shows up on
your existing Azure invoice.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:25.230
Second, your existing governance
policies just keep working.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.210
The same Azure Policy, Defender,

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:31.680
and DDoS protection you
already have applies to this.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.350
Third, multi-provider support.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:37.140
Bring whichever LLM
you've already deployed.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.290
Swapping providers is a
key change, not a rewrite.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:44.550
It's the same agent
configuration either way.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.830
Siemens is one of
our key deployments.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.690
Their field service
technicians interact

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.660
with manufacturing systems
through voice and video instead

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.150
of dashboards and
manual commands.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.850
The same architecture is
running customer service,

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:59.610
sales onboarding, and
corporate training.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:04.170
Identical runtime, but
a different agent.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.790
Let me show you how
this comes together,

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.950
from provisioning the
resource to the live agent

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:14.010
in your application in three
steps: create, deploy, monitor.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.510
Let's build one.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:19.140
Step one, create your agent.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:22.380
The Omniagent API is part
of your Azure account.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.330
You spin it up directly
from the Azure portal,

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:26.790
the same way you'd
create a Cosmos DB

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.800
or any other Azure resource.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:29.790
No separate purchase,

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.190
no separate vendor,
no separate bill.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.790
It lives alongside the
rest of your Azure stack.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:39.210
Once it's running, open the
Omniagent API dashboard.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:41.790
We'll walk through
it click by click.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.470
Everything you see
here is also available

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.620
through the REST API itself, so
you can build it click by click

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:49.380
or call it from your code.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.710
A quick word on the anatomy of
an agent before we build one.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:54.030
There are four parts.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:58.020
The Omniagent is the face, the
voice, and the personality.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.030
The knowledge is the
documents and data

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:02.490
that the agent grounds
its answers in.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.010
FAQs are the curated
questions and answers

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:08.490
for things you want handled
the same way every time.

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:11.700
And tools are what the agent
calls out to when it needs

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.190
to take action on
the user's behalf.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:17.670
Omniagent plus knowledge
plus FAQs plus tools.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.440
That's your agent.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.900
Now, let's go and build one.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.000
On the dashboard,
you'll see two buttons:

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.790
New Omniagent and
New Digital Twin.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:30.180
An Omniagent is a fictional
agent for support, sales,

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.270
onboarding, or any role
inside your application.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.520
A digital twin is
an agent configured

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.090
to represent a specific person:
their likeness, their voice,

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:42.170
their style, their
personal knowledge.

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.240
You don't even have to
start from scratch.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.060
Napster ships a library
of stock agents,

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.940
pre-built personas
across common roles.

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:53.510
Pick one, customize
it, and you're off.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:56.190
Or, build your own from scratch.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:05:00.150
Today, we're building Vera,
a field service specialist.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:03.840
We give her a name, upload a
photo, and the system spins

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.930
up a lifelike video avatar.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:08.970
Then, we wire up the rest.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:10.880
We attach knowledge.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.160
Vera already has the
equipment service manuals,

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:16.160
the parts catalog, and the
diagnostic procedures.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.950
Today, we're adding the
Atlas service knowledge.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:27.930
We add FAQs.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:30.240
She already has the
equipment safety protocols

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.520
and the parts and warranty FAQ.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:35.680
Today, we're adding
the field service FAQ.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.530
And we attach tools.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:53.040
This is what Vera uses to take
action on the user's behalf.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:56.370
She already has get service
history, look up part,

00:05:56.370 --> 00:06:00.240
order part, schedule inspection,
and escalate to engineering.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.250
A pretty comprehensive toolbox.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:05.280
But today, we're going to
add get customer lookup,

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.210
so she can resolve a
customer ID into a name.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:12.900
Omniagent, knowledge,
FAQs, tools.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.540
That's Vera, defined once.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:19.570
But before we ship her, we
test her in the playground.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.720
VERA: Hi, how can I help you?

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:28.290
Marcus: Hey, Vera.

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:31.260
I'm on site at customer 4421.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:33.000
The compressor is short-cycling.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.160
What should I check?

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.150
VERA: Hey, Marcus.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:37.560
That's Northgate Industrial,

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:39.750
same compressor we
worked on last month.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.360
Their model has a known
high-pressure switch issue.

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.130
Let me pull their
service history.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.860
Three pressure-related
work orders in six months.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.900
Want me to open a parts request?

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.560
Marcus: Yeah, go ahead.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:53.130
VERA: Done.

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.800
The switch will be at your local
depot by tomorrow morning.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.890
I've also scheduled a follow-up
inspection for 30 days out.

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:01.410
This unit needs
closer monitoring.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:03.040
Marcus: Thanks, Vera.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:05.940
VERA: Good luck
with the install.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.380
Call back if anything comes up.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.460
ZIV NAVOTH: That's the
same Vera we just built.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:16.230
When she behaves the way we
want, we can move on to deploy.

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:18.300
The agent is
multimodal by design,

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.900
so it ships across the channels
your users actually use.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.200
We also refer to this
as an Omniagent.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:28.650
On the web, the web SDK
opens a WebRTC connection

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:31.770
and renders the video agent
inside your application

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:35.370
as a sales agent on your pricing
page, a support concierge

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:36.840
in your help center,

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.480
an onboarding guide
on your homepage.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.760
Lifelike presence,
real-time voice,

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.970
mounted to any
element on your page.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.340
In your native mobile
or desktop app,

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.650
the WebSocket handler
streams real-time voice

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:51.600
between the app and the agent.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:54.660
Voice first, no
browser required.

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:59.820
On the phone, the agent answers
inbound SIP and VoIP calls.

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:02.760
An after-hours support
line, an intake agent

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:06.840
on your main number, a callback
for high-priority customers.

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.990
Your customers dial a
regular phone number.

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:12.240
The agent picks up.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:13.740
On text, the agent routes

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:16.230
through your messaging
channel of your choice.

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:19.500
Same agent, same
memory, same tools.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:22.380
You can also turn on
memory at this layer.

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:24.000
When you start a
session for a user,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.310
you tell the Omniagent
API to remember.

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:29.100
From that point on, the
agent extracts facts,

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.900
summarizes conversations,

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:34.920
and carries that context
forward automatically.

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:38.040
Memory follows the user
across every channel.

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:40.140
Start the conversation
in your web app,

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.090
continue it on the phone,

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:44.280
pick it up over text
the next morning.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:49.680
Same agent, with the same
context, and no re-explaining.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.320
Step three: Monitor.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:53.910
Every conversation the agent has

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:57.660
on every channel is
captured and inspectable.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:00.630
Open any session, and you
see the full transcript,

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:04.290
every tool the agent called,
every memory read and write,

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:06.470
and the identity of the user.

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:10.310
End-to-end audit,
native to the platform.

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:11.960
Track the outcomes that matter.

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:13.850
How many issues did
the agent resolve?

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:15.620
How many actions did it take?

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:20.190
Iterate the prompt, redeploy,
and watch the numbers move.

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:21.360
That's the platform.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:24.750
A multimodal AI co-worker, you
can spin up in your Azure tenant

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:27.420
in minutes, running
on your own LLM,

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:30.000
deployed across every
channel your customers use,

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.820
and inspectable end-to-end.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.010
Three things for
you to take away.

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:37.140
First, it's available
today on Azure.

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:40.140
Provision it from the portal the
same way you'd provision any

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:42.030
other Azure resource.

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:44.820
Second, once you're signed up,
the quickstart will get you

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:47.430
to a running agent
in under 15 minutes.

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:51.200
Bring your existing Azure OpenAI
deployment, and you're off.

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:53.000
And third, if you're
here at Build,

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.350
come find us at our booth.

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:57.470
We'll build a co-worker
with you live.

00:09:57.470 --> 00:09:59.250
Thanks for your time.
